ers. \n\nSo between F1 and J2. J2 has three unshown, so discarding it is less risky. F1 is the last one. \n\nSo maybe discard F1. But I also have F2 in my hand. If I discard F1, someone might need it for a pung. But if I keep F1, maybe they can't get it. \n\nAnother angle: My hand has F1 and F2. If I discard F1, I still have F2. Maybe I can use F2 for something else. \n\nAlternatively, if I discard J2, I have F1 and F2. Maybe they can form a pair if I get another. But J2 is a single tile. \n\nConsidering all this, discarding F1 might be the best option because it's the last one and could block a potential pung. So the answer would be Play F1.\n", "output": "Evaluation: Discard F1 to block potential pung for others and maintain flexibility in hand.\nReason: F1 is the last unshown wind tile, reducing risk of completing others' melds. Keeping B7/B8/B9 supports potential chow, and W7 redundancy allows safe discard.\nAnswer: Play F1"}
