ting across has a chow of T3, T4, T5 and another chow. If I discard T7, maybe they can use it for a chow? But T7 is part of T7, T8, T9? The player across has T8 in their melds. Not sure. T7 might be safe to discard.\n\n5. **Play J3**: J3 is a dragon tile. Unshown J3 has 3 left. The player across has a J3 in their history. Discarding J3 could be useful if I need to form a pair or pung of dragons. But since I have only one J3, and the melds don't involve dragons except maybe for a pair. However, I already have a J3 in my hand. If I discard it, maybe others can use it. But since there are three left, maybe safe.\n\nNow, considering my hand. My current hand has W8, W9, T7, B6, J3. The melds include a kong of W1, pung of B9, and a chow of W4-6. To win, I need to form a hand worth at least 8 points. Let's see what possible combinations I can have.\n\nLooking at my current tiles: I have W8 and W9, which are terminals. B6 is a middle tile. J3 is a dragon. T7 is a middle tile. The melds are already set. To form a winning hand, I need to draw or discard to get the right combination. \n\nIf I keep W8 and W9 as terminals, and T7 and B6 as part of chows or pungs. But I don't have other tiles to form those. Maybe if I discard B6, I can keep more terminals. Alternatively, keeping J3 could help if I need a pair or pung of dragons. But I only have one J3. \n\nLooking at the scoring patterns. If I can form a hand with terminals and honors, that might give me points. For example, All Terminals and Honors is worth 32 points. But I need to have only terminals and honors. My current hand has W8, W9 (terminals), J3 (honor), and T7, B6 (non-terminals). So if I discard T7 and B6, maybe I can get to all terminals and honors. But I need to see if that's possible.\n\nAlternatively, if I discard B6, I can focus on keeping the terminals. Let's see. If I discard B6, my hand becomes W8, W9, T7, J3. Then I need to draw or discard to get another tile. But the melds are already set. Wait, the melds a