one tile now. So maybe discard T7 first, then B6 in a future turn. But since the user can only discard one tile now, they need to choose which one to discard.\n\nAlternatively, if the user discards B6, they can keep W8, W9 (terminals), T7, J3. Then, if they can get rid of T7 in a future turn, they can approach All Terminals and Honors. But T7 is a middle tile. Alternatively, if they discard T7, they keep B6, which is a middle tile. Neither is ideal.\n\nAnother possibility is to look for other patterns. The user has a concealed kong of W1, which is worth points. Also, the pung of B9 is exposed (from Peng), so that's an exposed meld. The chow of W4-6 is exposed (from Chi). \n\nThe user's hand includes J3 (White Dragon). If they can form a pair of dragons, that might help. But they only have one J3. If they discard J3, they lose the chance to form a pair of dragons. But since they have only one, it's not a pair. \n\nLooking at the unshown tiles, J3 has 3 left. So if the user keeps J3, there's a chance someone else might discard it, allowing the user to form a pair. But since the user is the one discarding, maybe keeping J3 is better for potential points.\n\nAnother angle: the user has a concealed kong of W1, which is worth points. The pung of B9 is exposed. The chow of W4-6 is exposed. The user's hand has W8, W9 (terminals), T7, B6, J3. \n\nIf the user can form a hand with All Terminals and Honors, that's 32 points. To do that, they need to discard T7 and B6. But since they can only discard one now, they need to choose which one. If they discard B6, they still have T7, which is a non-terminal. If they discard T7, they still have B6, a non-terminal. So neither is ideal. \n\nAlternatively, maybe the user is aiming for a different pattern. For example, if they can form a hand with a pair of J3 and some other melds. But they only have one J3. \n\nLooking at the melds, the user has a kong of W1, which is a concealed kong. Concealed kongs are worth points. Also, the user has